      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the status of patients 10 years after referral for coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective analysis of case notes from all patients referred between 1 April 1981 and 31 March 1985. Full information gathered from hospital notes, GP records, and Registrar General for Scotland."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "District General Hospital, West Lothian, Scotland."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "102 patients referred for CABG during study period. Cardiac surgery was undertaken in Brompton Hospital, London, Royal Infirmary, Edinburgh, and Western Infirmary, Glasgow."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "At 10 years after operation 32 patients had died (27 cardiac, five non-cardiac causes). Full data were not available for five patients. Of the 65 remaining patients 24 had no angina, 13 had had a repeat procedure (CABG or angioplasty), and 28 had angina."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Long term benefits of CABG surgery is disappointing. Further steps are required to reduce progression of disease in this population."
        }
      ]},
